            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"An idiomatic expression symbolizing extreme mortal peril, profound gloom, the experience of dying, or (especially in prophetic and New Testament contexts) the spiritual darkness and hopelessness of humanity without God.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A typological 'Way' or pattern of the world that is shaped by the substance of Death (comprising sin, darkness, and bondage), which stands in structural opposition to the 'shadow of Christ.'\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A poetic metaphor or idiom for deep physical or spiritual darkness and life-threatening danger.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A typological pattern ('shadow') cast by the spiritual substance of Death.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus applies the term to situational experiences of mortal peril, literal dying, or spiritual blindness. The book replaces this with a systematic, typological framework where 'shadow' literally means a patterned shape cast by a corresponding 'substance' (Death).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus understands 'shadow of death' (translating the Hebrew 'tsalmaveth') as a unified idiom or compound word meaning deep darkness or severe peril. The book dissects the English phrase into a literal 'shadow' (a typological pattern) and 'death' (its substance), generating a novel cosmological dichotomy ('Shadow of Death' vs. 'Shadow of Christ') absent from the consensus.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book redefines the term by applying a specific typological definition of 'shadow' (as a pattern reflecting a substance) to the word 'death'. This departs completely from the term's function as a situational idiom for gloom or danger.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"INSUFFICIENT\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The Hebrew term 'tsalmaveth' is widely recognized by linguists and commentators as an idiom or root for 'deep darkness' or 'thick gloom.' Interpreting it by splitting the English rendering into a typological 'shadow' cast by a 'substance' of death imposes an artificial framework onto a poetic Hebrew expression, ignoring its original linguistic and contextual usage.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
